course: dictionary
lesson: 4419
title: Words that travel
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A hamburger is a flat round cake of minced beef, fried or grilled and served in a bread bun, often with salad, cheese and sauce. The words around it give you clues: «a hamburger bun», «a hamburger and chips», «veggie burger». Each phrase shows you the bun, the chips or the veggie kind is part of the same food family.
explain: Splitting a word into parts can be a tempting guess. Ham plus burger sounds like meat in a bun, but that is not where the word comes from. The sentence «"Hamburger" comes from Hamburg, a city in Germany, not from ham» shows that the guess is wrong. When a guess might be wrong, check a dictionary or ask a grown-up.
explain: The meaning can change from place to place. In the USA, hamburger can also mean the minced beef before it is cooked. Context tells you which meaning is meant: a hamburger you eat is the cooked cake, but a hamburger at the butcher's counter is the raw mince.
fact: A hamburger is a flat round cake of minced beef in a bread bun.
fact: «Hamburger» comes from Hamburg, a city in Germany, not from ham.
fact: People split the word into «ham» and «burger», then made new words like «cheeseburger» and «veggie burger».
fact: In the USA, hamburger can also mean the minced beef before it is cooked.
fact: Health lessons say hamburgers are food to eat sometimes, not every day.
exercise: «He ordered a hamburger and chips.» What is a hamburger? | *A flat round cake of minced beef in a bread bun | A type of bread with no filling | A small green vegetable | A city in Germany
exercise: Where does the word hamburger come from? | An American football team | Ham and burger, two words joined together | A German word for a bread bun | *Hamburg, a city in Germany
exercise: In the USA, hamburger can also mean what? | A fizzy drink | *The minced beef before it is cooked | A bread bun with no filling | A type of cheese
test: A friend says «I want a veggie burger, please.» What is the clue that this is a different kind of burger? | *The word veggie | The word want | The word I | The word please
test: Why is splitting the word into ham and burger a wrong guess for its origin? | *Because it comes from Hamburg, a city in Germany | Because ham is a kind of cheese | Because hamburgers have never had meat in them | Because burger is a word for a bread bun in German
exam: In the USA, a shopper at the butcher's asks for «a hamburger». What might they want? | A cooked cake in a bread bun | A bowl of salad | A hot drink | *Minced beef before it is cooked
